On 2008-12-04, H Vlems <hvlems@freenet.de> wrote:
> So there's no support for the RX33 or RX50, unless you're prepared to
> write your own driver.

There are a couple of possibilities.

The primary difference between the RX33 and RX23 from the controller's
perspective is that the RX33 changes speed based upon the state of one
of the interface signals (I think it's pin 2). The drive has a lot of
jumpers, though, so it should be possible to jumper the drive to stay at
300 RPM and have the Alpha think the RX33 is an RX23. However, the Alpha
isn't going to know what to do with an RX50 floppy, since it needs to be
read at a lower data rate, on only one side, and using a software
interleave.

If you have a SCSI controller and one of DEC's SCSI floppies,
there may be a way out. It's not advertised, but the SCSI floppy
controller supports the RX33 drive as well as the RX23. The controller
tells the difference between the two drives using the drive select
(i.e., at power-up it homes the drives; which drive select succeeds
determines which drive is present). Only one drive may be attached, but
it may be either an RX23 or RX33 (the SCSI RX26 controller supports RX23
and RX33 as well as RX26).

However, earlier firmware versions don't handle software interleave on 
RX50s; there were a variety of software interleaves in use and it wasn't 
clear which one should be done. Later ones handle a software interleave, 
but I forget which one. So even then you may wind up needing to copy the 
whole disk and unshuffle the blocks to get to the data.
-- 
roger ivie
rivie@ridgenet.net


