On 17 dec, 23:55, "Robert Jarratt" <nos...@nosp.am> wrote:
> I have a MicroVAX Model 80 for which I would like to get some more memory to
> replace some failed modules. How proprietary is the memory? Might I be able
> to use some non-DEC memory if I can find it? If so what spec does it need to
> be?
>
> Thanks
>
> Rob

Third party memory is available for most of the microVAX models. Your
system is (I guess) a microVAX 3100 model 80.
According to the specs (found here:
http://h18002.www1.hp.com/alphaserver/vax/archive/mv3100_3040_8090.html)
the 3100-80 can handle 72 MB memory. Considering its cpu speed you
might be able to buy a used VAXstation 4000 model 60
or even a model 90 or 90A for the same amount of money you're now may
be willling to spend on memory.
A 4000-60 can have 72 MB memory, the -90(A) up to 128 MB. They run
considerably faster than a 3100-80.
I found this document on the 3100-80: http://deathrow.vistech.net/~cvisors/DEC94MDS/a0511mg1.pdf
The 3100-80 uses MS44-xA memory which is also used in the VAXstation
4000 models. Note that the memory boards must be installed in pairs.
On eBay MS44-DA is priced at US$42 and MS44-CA at half that price.
Hans



