On Dec 24, 6:54=A0pm, Charles Richmond <friz...@tx.rr.com> wrote:
> It was dot matrix, but it
> seemed to me that *some* dots of characters were "in between"
> where the regular matrix was.

This was true of the regular DECwriter font for the LA36, not just the
APL font.

While the first DECwriter, which was upper-case only, used the normal
5 x 7 dot matrix arrangement, the LA36 used what is usually called a 7
x 7 dot-matrix font; the basic matrix for characters was 4 x 7, and so
if printing at halfway dot positions was not possible, characters like
the letter "m" would be impossible to print in a recognizable fashion.

Of course, later versions of the DECwriter used more attractive and
improved fonts.

John Savard


