> printers were not actually a "matrix" printer, rather a "vector"
> printer. =A0The printing was accomplished by a 1 x 7 matrix of little
> hammers which would fire into a ribbon.

Dot matrix printers typically had only a vertical column of printing
elements on the print head. Vector plotters, like those made by
Calcomp, had *one* pen, which was used to draw lines.

So having 7 print solenoids and not 35 would not have led to it being
called a "vector printer" in those days.

But you are quite correct that the head moved at such a speed as to
preclude overlapping dots from being printed.

John Savard


