On Dec 28, 3:21=A0am, H Vlems <hvl...@freenet.de> wrote:

> The DECwriter 120 Mk III was available with an APL keyboard lay-out
> IIRC.
> Is that the DECwriter model you were referring to?

Given his description of the characters, as looking like dot-matrix
characters, but allowing dots in obvious half-way positions, I think
it's clear he was referring to the more primitive LA36 Decwriter II,
which did have dots in half-way positions, but not the dense high-
quality printing of later Decwriter models.

Before the fancy smaller Decwriter, there was a later model that still
looked a lot like an LA36, though. I forget whether it printed the
same way, so you could be referring to an equivalent model for the
purposes of this discussion.

John Savard



