On Dec 28, 3:34=A0pm, Charles Richmond <friz...@tx.rr.com> wrote:
> Mensanator wrote:
> > On Dec 28, 5:21 am, Jane Sullivan <j...@yddraiggoch.demon.co.uk>
> > wrote:
> >> In message
> >> <7fca9947-374c-4350-b030-84b29731d...@x16g2000prn.googlegroups.com>,
> >> Mensanator <mensana...@aol.com> writes
>
> >>> the printer was
> >>> incapable of legibly printing the phrase
> >>> "all mississippi llamas live in illinois"
> >> Why would anyone want to print that?
>
> > Because I can.
>
> > Seriously, that's what System Test is all about,
> > deliberately provoking failure. Our memory diagnostic
> > had a test called Core Heating, accessing the memory
> > so furiously that it made the cores literally get hot.
> > Why would anyone want to do that? They wouldn't, but
> > since heat affects magnetism, it's a worse case test.
>
> > Likewise, the above phrase is a worse case test for
> > stupidly written printer drivers.
>
> Does this test have anything to do with all the double letters???
> Are double letters what cause the problem here???

No, but double letters exaberate the problem if the
problem letters are the ones doubled. It was caused
by the printer driver thinking it had to make
adjustments to character spacing for proportional
fonts (not being aware of the fact that DRAFT mode
used a monospace font). And I know it was the
print driver because when I hooked up a data scope,
I saw it ending incremental head motion controls
instead of letting the printer use it's default
spacing for monospaced fonts. And all the stupid
head motion controls made the machine slow down
so that draft mode printed slower than quality
mode.

It THOUGHT that "i" and "l" were composed of a
single column of dots and sent more spacing than
required (none at all should have been sent as a
monospced font takes care of that).

The more "i" and "l" characters inside a word,
the worse the problem. Wide characters like "m"
didn't help either.

So double letters as in "all" were a problem, as
well as quadruplets like "illinois". The double
"s" and "p" were less significant than all the "i"
in "mississippi".

That phrase has 16 "i" and "l" and 18 other letters
(including some "m"). And it's goofy enough to stick
in your head as evidenced that I still remember it
even though I haven't used said printer in 25 years.

