> > follow the "cells" of the dot matrix. It had "'tween" dots.)

> Way, way back, in the 1970s, in the days of the Byte Magazine "Write
> an APL Interpreter" competition, I managed to do a usable
> representation of them all on an LA36 by backspace and overstrike, so
> it must be possible to do without the tweenies.

More specifically in reply to your point:

If you create a character that looks like the one you want by
backspace and overstrike, it does not follow that printing this
character in a single pass of the printhead wouldn't violate the rule
against two dots in adjacent, 1/2-dot apart, print positions being
present in the same character.

And dots in odd 1/2 dot locations were part of the regular LA36 font,
not just the APL font, as I've noted.

John Savard


